A||||5||hugh||leftovers,what do you do with them?||||7||
Z||000000||hugh||11-22-2001||09:40 PM||||After my post meal nap,I cut off most of the easy meat to get at on the turkey and wrap it for use later in sandwiches,etc.Then I fill a big pot most of the way with water,put in everything left of the turkey.I bring it to a boil then back off the heat and simmer it for a few hours.When all the meat is falling off the bones I turn it off.Then I take all the turkey parts out of the pot and pick all the meat off and disgard the remains.In the meantime the broth is cooling so I can skim the fat off.After skimming the fat,I put in the amount of turkey I want to use and add whatever vegetables are handy.Now I have a soup that is better than any you can buy.You can also thicken it and get premade pie crusts,crisp the crust in the oven,then put in the thickened broth,cover with another crust or those biscuits that come in the dairy case at the store.Heat till the crust is done and you have some great turkey pot pie.<BR> That's it for today's cooking lesson!<BR>How about some more Ideas? ||||reg||7
Z||000001||JSocha||11-22-2001||10:28 PM||||I pick off what ever turkey meat that I can off the carcus.  Then I wrap the left over carcus, skin and any fat of the turkey in cheese cloth with slices of fresh ginger, celery and whole green onions.<P>Then I place the cheese cloth sack in boiling water (big 16 to 20 quart stock pot) and boil it for one to two days and/or until the bones turn to mush or will crumble when you squeeze them.  I also allow about 1/4 to 1/2 of the water to evaporate during this time to concentrate the broth.<P>Afterward, I remove the cheese cloth sack, either throw it in the garbage or take the sack out to the cats at our barn and let them feast on it, the meat that came off and the bones as they are soft enough for them to digest.<P>In the meantime, I freeze the turkey broth that I now have for future quick soups and/or when I make Chinese food or other dishes as a substitue to chicken broth (which BTW is is also the way I make that broth...only I use real inexpensive whole chickens and just throw all the meat to the cats afterwards when its done).<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||Mach10||11-22-2001||10:43 PM||||And here you have the solution to world-hunger... Buy each country a turkey... They'll have leftovers to feed em for WEEKS  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Either give 'em to the Dog, or, if they are good, I'll keep 'em for lunch...||||reg||10
Z||000003||maryjane||11-22-2001||10:50 PM||||stick em in the back of the fridge and grow bacteria cultures?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Jane usually makes 'mustgo soup' out of them.<BR>Jsocha- can I come be one of the cats in your barn? sounds like they have it pretty good.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
